# The first king, when the sarcophagus is opened. Fired from
# JobDriver_TSC_OpenKingsTomb. He is the only authority the crown cannot
# spin: the only man who ever wore it whole.
#
# This scene delivers the truth and then hands the player the campaign's
# last decision (crown_ending). {PLAYER} is whoever opened the lid.
#
# Two openings: campaigns with the regalia fetch (TSC_RegaliaGathered)
# get the waking rite made explicit - the three pieces on the lid are
# what he runs on, and he says so. Legacy saves that never had the
# fetch keep the old opening. Both converge on -> who / -> talking.

dialogue TSC_Dialogue_FirstKing

entry start_woken if flag(TSC_RegaliaGathered)

== start_woken ==
The lid grinds back. Inside is a small dry man in the remains of very good clothes, hands folded on a hollow where a crown should sit.

On the lid where the party laid them: the ring, gone past warm; the staff, its crescent turned some invisible force to face the man below; and the amulet, silent. Whatever it has been counting toward since the funeral, that moment has arrived.

He is nine hundred years dead, and nothing in him moves. Then the ring flares once, like a held breath let go, and he opens his eyes.

* [Wait.] -> woke
* "Can you hear me?" -> woke

== woke ==
"Ah." His voice is a page turning. He does not look at the party first. He looks at the three things on his own lid, one at a time, taking inventory.

"The sun to keep my hours. The ring to keep my name. The tool to hold me in a shape." A dry finger moves from one to the next as he says it. "A when, a who, and a what. Take any one of the three off that lid and the other two have nobody to argue about."

"They carried them out of here behind my coffin, so that nobody could ever wake me by accident, and you have walked all three back and set them out in the right order."

"Understand what you have done, because you have earned the understanding: I am not alive. I am brought back, but only just. While they lie there, I am awake. Carry them off, and I go back to being still."

The dry eyes come up at last.

"You did not walk three roads to return an old man's jewelry. It found somebody else."

* "You are the first king." -> who
* "It has been talking to us." -> talking
* [Wait.] -> who

== start ==
The lid grinds back. Inside is a small dry man in the remains of very good clothes, hands folded on a hollow where a crown should sit.

He is nine hundred years dead. He opens his eyes anyway.

"Ah," he says. His voice is a page turning. "It found somebody else."

* "You are the first king." -> who
* "It has been talking to us." -> talking
* [Wait.] -> who

== who ==
"I was a chieftain with a good year and a thing I dug out of this hill," he says, "and then I was the first king, and then I was a lesson nobody learned. In that order, and faster than you would think."

He does not try to sit up. Whatever twisted magic animates him does not allow it.

"They took it off my head and buried me under it, and then my son wore it. And then a cousin. Eleven of them inside a hundred years, every one of them brilliant and every one of them used up before forty, and not one of them able to say why."

"It took the last of them burning her own hall down to end it. They broke the thing and sent five riders five directions, and they started them from this barrow, because this is where I found it, and they had run out of ideas about where else it belonged."

"I have had a very long time to work out what I would say to whoever put it back together. Would you like the short version or the true one?"

* "The true one." -> truth
* "The short one." -> short
* "We came through the bride's tower. The chair at the top faces this hill." -> vigil
    if flag(TSC_VigilRead)

== vigil ==
He is quiet for longer than a dead man needs to be.

"She would not come down," he says at last. "They sealed the mound and read the rites and she went up that tower, and the last thing I heard as the stone closed was her voice telling them she could see it fine from there."

"Nine hundred years I have wondered whether she stayed. You have just told me she did. Whatever else you came down here to take or to ask, you have already left more than you will carry out."

* "She kept your ring, too. We carried it here." -> vigil2

== vigil2 ==
"Then it is all of it home," he says. "Her watch, my ring, and the thing that cost us both. That is the whole household back under one roof, at last, and nobody left standing on a tower."

A pause.

"Now. The short version, or the true one?"

* "The true one." -> truth
* "The short one." -> short

== short ==
"Do not wear it."

A pause.

"That is the short version. It is completely useless, because every man who has ever been told it was already carrying the thing, and carrying it is most of the way to wearing it. Have the true one instead."

* [The true one.] -> truth

== talking ==
"Of course it has. In your own voice, I expect, and reasonably." Something that might be pity crosses the dry face. "It did that to me on a hillside, when I was twenty-six and had just won something I did not deserve to win."

* "What is it?" -> truth

== truth ==
"It is not a curse and it is not a god. It is a *want*. Somebody very old made a thing that could want, in this hill, before there was anybody in these valleys worth putting it on, and they did not think about what it would want. The answer turned out to be: to be worn, and through the wearing, to be everything."

He lets that sit.

"It kept every promise it made me. Every one. The kingdom I asked for, I got. The enemies, gone. The harvests, good. And every year there was less of ME making the decisions and more of IT, until at the end my own men could not tell which of us had given an order, and neither could I. I did not die of a blade. I ran out."

"Then it did the same to eleven more in turn, and each of them thought they were the one who would be careful."

His fingers move very slightly on the hollow at his chest.

"Nine hundred years of lying here has bought me one piece of advice worth the trip. Do you want it?"

* "Yes." -> advice
* "Will it help?" -> advice

== advice ==
"It wants without stopping, and it has wanted the same thing since the day it was split: to be whole, and worn. That part never sleeps and you will not talk it out of it. What it cannot do is reach. It has no hands and it has never had any, and that is its one weakness and it is total. Eight centuries of manoeuvring pieces of itself across three graves, a bandit's counting room and a courier who could not stop walking, and every single step of that had to be walked by somebody else."

The dry eyes come up.

"So the only question that has ever mattered is the one you are about to answer, standing over my coffin with all five pieces and nobody left to stop you. Not whether you are strong enough to hold it. Whether you can put it down."

* [Decide.] -> choice

== choice ==
Four pieces in the company's packs and the fifth from the road, and the hollow in the dead king's hands exactly the shape of all of them together.

Nobody says anything. It is not that kind of moment.

* [Put it together. Wear it.] -> wear
* [Break the roads open again: five pieces, five directions, five new riders.] -> scatter
* [Lay it in the coffin with him and seal the barrow.] -> entomb
* [Leave it exactly where it lies. Walk out. Tell no one.] -> leave
* "Your wardens tried to kill it, and failed. We think we know why they failed." -> unmake_ask
* [Leave him, for now.] -> end

== unmake_ask ==
For the first time since the lid came off, the dead man moves: one finger, lifting off the hollow at his chest, pointing at whoever spoke.

"Say what you think you know."

* [Lay out everything the company has gathered.] -> unmake_agree
    if flag(TSC_UnmakeReady)
* [Lay out what the company has.] -> unmake_refuse
    if not flag(TSC_UnmakeReady)

== unmake_refuse ==
He hears it out to the end, and the finger comes down.

"Pieces. You have pieces of an argument, and this is not a thing you argue with half a case. My wardens had courage, numbers, and the staff itself, and it beat them, because a broken thing is still a made thing and they could not say *why* it had to end."

"Unmaking wants two witnesses. The five roads, whole, read off the walls of this barrow: what it did, in order, to everyone who carried it. And men of mine, on their feet, unbought and unbeaten, to stand for the house while it is done."

"Carry both, and ask me again. I have waited nine hundred years. I can wait out your reading."

* [Step back from the coffin.] -> choice

== unmake_agree ==
It takes a while to lay out, and he does not interrupt: the five roads, read whole off his own walls. And outside the door, on their feet, five men who yielded without being beaten.

When it is done, the chamber is very quiet.

"Nine hundred years," he says, "and twelve kings, and five roads, and nobody ever once asked me for *this*."

* "Can it be done?" -> unmake_how

== unmake_how ==
"It can. It could always be done, and only ever here, and only ever whole: it can only be unmade while it is one, and it can only die in the room it was born in. Which is below this floor, behind that wall, where the maker left it."

"The wardens shattered it, and then tried to destroy the pieces. They could not."

The dry eyes close for a moment.

"Understand the price before you say yes. The moment it knows, it will spend everything it has, and everything it has is in this room. My sons are in these boxes, and it has never once let go of anything it wore."

* "Open the room. We will stand the cost." -> unmake_go
* [Step back. Not yet.] -> choice

== unmake_go ==
"Then steel out, all of you," the dead king says, "and somebody keep a hand on my lid. It is about to be loud."

His fingers spread flat on the hollow at his chest, and behind the sarcophagus, stone that was never a door remembers that it is one.

* [Stand ready.] -> end
    do begin_unmaking()

== wear ==
The pieces go together without a seam, letters closing into a word, and it is not heavy at all.

The dead king watches with an expression that has no anger in it whatsoever. Of the available expressions, it is the worst.

"There it is," he says. "Thirteen." He does not sound surprised, or angry, or even especially disappointed. "Do not be too hard on yourself. I lasted nine years, and I was the best of twelve."

Whatever happens after this, it will be told about for a long time, and the tellers will disagree about whose idea any of it was.

* [The Shattered Crown.] -> end
    do crown_ending(wear)

== scatter ==
The company splits the crown the way it was split the first time: five pieces, five roads, and this time the carriers will be told exactly what they are carrying before they set out.

Which leaves the question of who carries them.

* [Who rides?] -> scatter_guard
    if flag(TSC_HonorGuardStands)
* [Who rides?] -> scatter_hired
    if not flag(TSC_HonorGuardStands)

== scatter_guard ==
The oath-keepers ask for the roads before anybody else can offer.

They do not make a speech about it. One of them says "we saw the first five off," and another says "aye," and that is the whole of the discussion. Five of them, five pieces, five directions, and this time nobody is waiting at a door for them to come back, because there will be nobody left in the barrow to wait.

They take up the pieces without ceremony. They have all carried packs before.

"Better," the dead king says, and for the first time in the conversation he sounds like a man and not a lesson. "Mine at least know what they are holding. Mine will not be talked into putting it on."

Somewhere in the dark, five thin voices that have not been able to want anything for a very long time start wanting again, in five directions at once.

* [The Shattered Crown.] -> end
    do crown_ending(scatter)

== scatter_hired ==
Five riders who are not from this story, because the men whose job this was are lying on the floor of the chamber behind them and cannot be asked.

The carriers are told the truth, which is more than the first five got. Whether that helps is a question for somebody eight hundred years from now.

"It will work," the dead king says. "For about eight hundred years. Which is not nothing. Nothing is what I managed."

Somewhere in the dark, five thin voices that have not been able to want anything for a very long time start wanting again, in five directions at once.

* [The Shattered Crown.] -> end
    do crown_ending(scatter)

== entomb ==
The pieces go into the hollow in the dead king's hands, one at a time. They fit. The wanting rises so hard the chamber leans.

Then the lid goes back on, and the leaning stops.

"Good," says the first king, from inside, quite clearly, and then says nothing else ever again.

* [Go up.] -> entomb_kept
    if flag(TSC_HonorGuardStands)
* [Go up.] -> entomb_alone
    if not flag(TSC_HonorGuardStands)

== entomb_kept ==
Outside, the Honor Guard are already at work on the stone throat with the patience of men who have been waiting nine hundred years for a job to finish.

Nobody gives them an order. There was never anybody left to give them one, and they never needed it.

* [The Shattered Crown.] -> end
    do crown_ending(entomb)

== entomb_alone ==
Outside, the chamber is quiet in the particular way of a room where a fight has finished. The plate lies where it fell, and there is nobody left on this floor to seal anything.

So the company does it themselves: rubble into the stone throat, hour after hour, until the way down is a hillside again. It is not a ceremony and nobody talks much. The men whose post this was would probably have done it faster.

* [The Shattered Crown.] -> end
    do crown_ending(entomb)

== leave ==
{PLAYER} closes the lid on an old man and his empty hands. Five pieces of a crown stay on the floor of a sealed room, at the bottom of a barrow nobody has found in eight hundred years.

The wanting follows them up the throat and out into the daylight, and then it stops, because nobody is carrying it.

Behind them, the mound is just a hill with good grass.

"Someone will find it," Wren says, eventually. Nobody argues. But it will not be them, and it will not be soon, and after five acts of this that turns out to be worth more than a better ending.

* [The Shattered Crown.] -> end
    do crown_ending(leave)
